window.DOCS = [{"slug": "overview", "title": "Overview", "path": "README.md", "md": "# Crypto Macro Fair-Value Model\n\nHow much USD can become crypto market cap? This project follows the dollars: how much USD is flowing, how much of it is locked into the crypto market and lent on to the US Treasury, and what multiple the market puts on each locked dollar. It reads the result against the Federal Reserve and Wall Street.\n\nIt has three parts:\n\n- **A web app and portal** in [site/](site/index.html): the chain of dollars today, scenario forecasts for total crypto and Bitcoin, and the docs. Static files, no backend.\n- **An Excel workbook**, [docs/crypto_model_v2.xlsx](docs/crypto_model_v2.xlsx) (v2.2): the same chain and driver model on its `Flow` sheet, plus a regression of crypto market cap on M2 and US equity wealth used as a cross-check. Every result is a live formula.\n- **A toolset** in [scripts/](scripts/): fetch new data, add a quarter, verify, rebuild the site.\n\nNot investment advice.\n\n## The chain today\n\n| Step | Measure | 2026 Q3 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Flowing USD | M2 money stock | $23.4T |\n| 2. Free USD | M2 − GDP ÷ V*: not needed by the real economy at normal velocity | $0.64T |\n| 3. Locked USD | USD stablecoin supply: dollars that entered crypto through the primary market | $304B (1.30% of M2) |\n| 3b. Treasuries behind it | Locked USD × share of reserves in Treasuries (assumed 75%) | $228B |\n| 4. Crypto V | Market cap ÷ locked USD: what trading makes of each locked dollar | 9.7× |\n| Result | Crypto market cap = V × locked USD | $2.96T |\n\nThe app's default forecast, the driver model, lets the Fed move locked USD through M2 and Wall Street move crypto V through equities. Tested out of sample it is a translator, not an oracle: told where equities went, it was 3% more accurate than assuming no change; not told, it was no better. A typical one-quarter error is about 26%.\n\n## Quick start\n\n```bash\nmake serve\n```\n\nthen open http://localhost:8765. Or open `site/index.html` directly from disk.\n\n## What the regression cross-check says\n\n| | 2026 Q3 |\n|---|---|\n| Actual crypto market cap | $2.96T |\n| Fair value given M2 and US equities | $5.2T (±1 SE band: $3.7T to $7.3T) |\n| Valuation gap | about −43% |\n| Real-time Z-score | −2.1 (\"cheap vs macro\") |\n| Liquidity regime | Neutral (policy rate 0.25pp lower than a year earlier, after the September 2026 hike) |\n\n**Read this with the next table in mind.** The same signal, tested on the 15 quarters where it could be tested out of sample, shows no timing skill:\n\n| Out-of-sample check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Average next-quarter return after \"Cheap\" (4 quarters) | +13% |\n| Average next-quarter return after \"Neutral\" (11 quarters) | +13% |\n| Correlation of Z-score with next-quarter return | −0.13 (t ≈ −0.5, not significant) |\n| Directional hit rate | 53% |\n\nSo the gap is real as a description (crypto fell 47% from 2025 Q3 to 2026 Q2 while M2 and US equities made new highs), but the data cannot tell you whether it closes by crypto rising, by equities falling, or not at all. It is context, not a trade signal.\n\nThese figures are also on the workbook's `Summary` sheet and on the portal, where they update themselves.\n\n## Repository layout\n\n| Path | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| [site/](site/index.html) | Portal, forecast app and docs viewer |\n| [docs/crypto_model_v2.xlsx](docs/crypto_model_v2.xlsx) | The workbook |\n| [docs/methodology.md](docs/methodology.md) | Both models: equations, theory, estimates, forecast test, limitations, references |\n| [docs/web-app.md](docs/web-app.md) | What the site shows and how it is put together |\n| [docs/toolset.md](docs/toolset.md) | The scripts, and how to add a quarter with them |\n| [docs/workbook-guide.md](docs/workbook-guide.md) | Sheet-by-sheet and column-by-column reference, named ranges, formula conventions |\n| [docs/update-guide.md](docs/update-guide.md) | The same update done by hand in Excel |\n| [docs/data-dictionary.md](docs/data-dictionary.md) | Every input series: definition, source, confidence grade |\n| [scripts/](scripts/) | `fetch_inputs.py`, `add_quarter.py`, `build_site.py`, `check_model.py` |\n| [Makefile](Makefile) | `make update`, `make check`, `make audit`, `make serve` |\n| [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md) | Version history |\n\n## Keeping it current\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/fetch_inputs.py \"2026 Q4\"\n```\n\nfetches seven of the eleven inputs from FRED, the Treasury and DefiLlama and writes a file with three values to fill in by hand. Then:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/add_quarter.py --from-json data/quarters/2026Q4.json\n```\n\n```bash\nmake update\n```\n\nThe first adds the quarter to the workbook and checks it; the second rebuilds the site. Details in the [toolset guide](docs/toolset.md).\n\n## Using the workbook\n\n1. Open the workbook and go to `Summary`. It shows the latest reading, the liquidity chain, the statistics that say how far to trust it, and nine integrity checks that should all read `OK`.\n2. To try your own assumptions, overwrite the yellow scenario row at the bottom of `Model`, or the yellow cells on `Params`.\n3. To add a new quarter, use `scripts/add_quarter.py`, or insert a row above the scenario row on both `Model` and `Flow`, copy the row above into it and type the new inputs. Nothing else needs editing. Details are in the [update guide](docs/update-guide.md).\n\n## The workbook at a glance\n\n| Sheet | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `Guide` | One-page instructions and colour legend |\n| `Summary` | Latest reading, scenario, trust statistics, integrity checks |\n| `Model` | Inputs, diagnostics, fair value, real-time signal. One row per quarter |\n| `Flow` | The liquidity chain and the driver model. Rows match `Model` |\n| `Params` | Editable assumptions (yellow), live regression output, range control |\n| `Backtest` | Out-of-sample performance of the signal |\n| `Charts` | Actual vs fair value with error band; real-time Z-score; valuation gap; locked USD; crypto V |\n| `Data_Sources` | Definition, source, link and confidence grade for every series |\n| `Audit_Log` | 14 logic/formula problems in the original sheet and 87 data corrections |\n| `Original_v1` | The original sheet, untouched |\n\nThe model in one line: `ln(Crypto) = a + b1·ln(M2) + b2·ln(Equity)`, estimated by `LINEST` on all actual quarters for the fair value, and re-estimated on earlier quarters only for the real-time signal. See [methodology](docs/methodology.md).\n\n## Checking the workbook\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/check_model.py\n```\n\nThe script reads the blue input cells, recomputes the regression, the real-time signal and the backtest without using any spreadsheet formulas, and compares 29 results with the workbook's. It needs Python 3 and `openpyxl`. `make update` runs it for you.\n\n## What changed in v2.1\n\nv2.1 is a restructuring of v2. The model, the data and every reported number are the same.\n\n- **No more hand-edited ranges.** Estimation and backtest ranges are named ranges that end at the last actual quarter, so adding a quarter no longer means extending ranges in `Params` and `Backtest`.\n- **Row insertion is safe.** Formulas that look at the previous or next quarter now find it by position, so they stay correct when a row is inserted.\n- **The minimum estimation window is a live parameter.** In v2 changing it on `Params` had no effect.\n- **New `Summary` sheet** with the latest reading and integrity checks.\n- **Charts fixed.** The v2 charts plotted each value one quarter late. They were rebuilt, and a valuation-gap chart was added.\n\nFull list in the [changelog](CHANGELOG.md).\n\n## Limitations\n\nThe main ones, explained in [methodology](docs/methodology.md#limitations):\n\n- No model here beats \"no change\" at forecasting crypto market cap unless it is told where equities go.\n- Locked USD counts stablecoins only. The Treasury backing share is an assumption.\n- 28 quarters is a small sample, and only 15 can be tested out of sample.\n- Cointegration is not established, so there is no statistical evidence that gaps close.\n- The M2 coefficient also absorbs the crypto adoption trend.\n- No crypto-native variables (stablecoin supply, ETF flows, on-chain activity).\n- Data quality is uneven. Many pre-2025 values are rounded reference figures and some are interpolated. Check the grades in the [data dictionary](docs/data-dictionary.md) before relying on any single cell.\n\n"}, {"slug": "methodology", "title": "Methodology", "path": "docs/methodology.md", "md": "# Methodology\n\nHow the models work, why they are built this way, and where they should not be trusted.\n\nThere are two. The **macro regression** is described first. The **liquidity-flow model** and the **driver model** built on it are described [further down](#liquidity-flow-model). All three are in the workbook `crypto_model_v2.xlsx` (see the [workbook guide](workbook-guide.md)) and in the [web app](web-app.md), which adds multi-quarter scenarios and the out-of-sample comparison of forecasting methods.\n\n## Background: what v2 replaced\n\nThe original sheet ran a \"liquidity waterfall\": excess money, minus Treasury, gold and property absorption, minus what equities could absorb, times a rate multiplier, equals a target crypto market cap. That chain had a circular first step, mixed stocks with flows, and produced a target of exactly $0 in 21 of 29 quarters, so its \"valuation gap\" was just the actual market cap, and its Buy/Sell labels (typed by hand) often contradicted the numbers next to them.\n\nVersion 2 replaced the chain with an estimated relationship between crypto market cap, M2 and US equity wealth. It reports how uncertain that relationship is, generates signals by formula using only information available at the time, and tests them out of sample. The input data were also corrected (87 values). The 14 problems and all corrections are listed on the `Audit_Log` sheet.\n\n## Calculation steps\n\nAll steps are on the `Model` sheet, one row per quarter.\n\n### 1. Inputs\n\nM2, ON RRP (memo), nominal GDP, CPI YoY, Fed funds target (upper bound), total public debt, US equity market cap, crypto market cap, gold price, gold stock. Definitions and sources are in the [data dictionary](data-dictionary.md).\n\n### 2. Diagnostics\n\nThese give context. None of them enters the regression.\n\n- Gold market value = price × tonnes × 32,150.7 oz/t\n- Velocity `V = GDP / M2`\n- Money overhang ratio `= M2 × V* / GDP` and excess money `= M2 − GDP / V*`, where `V* = 1.45` is the 2015–2019 average velocity\n- Real policy rate `= rate − CPI`\n- Buffett ratio `= equity cap / GDP`\n- Crypto / M2\n- Quarterly change in public debt\n\n### 3. Fair value, full sample\n\n```\nln(Crypto) = a + b1·ln(M2) + b2·ln(Equity)\n```\n\nestimated by `LINEST` over all actual quarters. Then:\n\n- Fair value `= EXP(a + b1·ln M2 + b2·ln Equity)`\n- Band `= FV × EXP(±SE)`, where SE is the standard error of the regression in log points\n- Valuation gap `= actual / FV − 1`\n- Z-score `= residual / SE`\n\nThese columns use hindsight: the coefficients are fitted on the whole sample, including quarters after the one being valued.\n\n### 4. Real-time signal\n\nFor each quarter, the same regression is re-estimated with `TREND` / `LINEST` on **earlier quarters only**. This gives a fair value, standard error and Z-score that were knowable at the time. The first real-time quarter is the one with at least `Minimum estimation window` earlier quarters (12 by default, so 2022 Q4).\n\n- Z ≥ +1 → \"Rich vs macro\"; Z ≤ −1 → \"Cheap vs macro\"; otherwise \"Neutral\". The thresholds were set before looking at results and are not optimised.\n- Liquidity regime: Fed funds target up ≥ 0.5pp over four quarters → \"Tightening\"; down ≥ 0.5pp → \"Easing\". The nominal target is used so that an inflation spike is not mistaken for easing.\n\nThe real-time Z-score divides by the regression's standard error, not by a forecast standard error. The forecast error would be larger, especially early on, so the real-time Z-scores are somewhat overstated in absolute size.\n\n### 5. Backtest\n\nEach real-time signal is paired with the following quarter's realised crypto return. The `Backtest` sheet reports the average next-quarter return by signal, the correlation between Z-score and next-quarter return with its t-statistic, and a directional hit rate (sign of −Z equals sign of the return).\n\n### 6. Scenario row\n\nThe yellow row at the bottom of `Model` holds next-quarter inputs, carried forward from the latest quarter by default. Overwrite them with your assumptions. Leave crypto blank to see fair value only. The scenario row is never used in estimation or in the backtest.\n\n## Liquidity-flow model\n\nThe question it answers: how much USD can become crypto market cap? It follows the dollars.\n\n| Step | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Flowing USD | M2 |\n| Free USD | `M2 − GDP ÷ V*`, with V* = 1.45. Money beyond what the real economy needs at its normal velocity. This is the same anchored measure as the workbook's \"excess money\" |\n| Locked USD | Supply of USD stablecoins at quarter-end (DefiLlama) |\n| Lock-in share | Locked USD ÷ M2 |\n| Treasury collateral | Locked USD × the share of stablecoin reserves held in US Treasuries |\n| Crypto V | Crypto market cap ÷ locked USD |\n| Market cap | Crypto V × locked USD |\n\n**Primary and secondary market.** A dollar enters through the primary market when a stablecoin is minted against it. From then on it circulates in the secondary market, where trading sets prices. Crypto V is what that trading makes of each locked dollar. It is the counterpart of the velocity of M2, with market value in the place of GDP.\n\n**The dual lens.** A dollar that buys a stablecoin does not leave the banking system. The issuer spends it on Treasury bills, the cash goes to whoever sold the bills, and M2 is unchanged. What is created is a second claim: a dollar-token inside the crypto market, backed by a Treasury bill outside it. Locked USD therefore counts twice, as liquidity inside crypto and as demand for US government debt. In 2026 Q3, at an assumed 75% backing, issuers held about $228B of Treasuries, 0.57% of the debt stock. Their weight as a buyer depends on how fast locked USD is growing: it added about $70B in 2024 and $100B in 2025, but only $12B in the year to 2026 Q3.\n\n**What the identity can and cannot do.** `Market cap = V × locked USD` is true by definition, because V is defined as the ratio. It explains nothing on its own. Its use is to split a forecast into parts one can reason about separately: the money stock (the Fed), the share of it that crosses into crypto, and the multiple the market puts on it (Wall Street's appetite for risk).\n\n**History.** Locked USD rose from $4B (0.03% of M2) at the end of 2019 to $304B (1.30%) in 2026 Q3. Crypto V fell from 45× to about 10× over the same period, as the stablecoin base grew faster than market cap. Over the last 16 quarters V had a median of 11.1× and a middle half of 9.3× to 13.7×.\n\n**Free USD against locked USD.** Free USD peaked at $4.4T in 2020–2021 and has fallen to $0.64T as GDP caught up with the money stock. Locked USD kept rising throughout, from about 2% of free USD to 47%. So crypto's dollar base did not track the overhang quarter by quarter; it accumulated while the overhang was large and has not drained since.\n\n### Driver model\n\nThe default forecast in the web app. It lets the Fed and Wall Street move the chain:\n\n```\nLocked USD   = lock-in share × M2\nΔln V        = β · Δln(US equity cap) + κ · (average ln V − last ln V)\nMarket cap   = V × locked USD\n```\n\n- The Fed acts through M2, and so through locked USD.\n- Wall Street acts through β: crypto V rises and falls with equities.\n- κ pulls V back toward its own average.\n- The lock-in share holds unless the user sets a drift.\n\nEstimated on 26 quarters: β = 0.69, κ = 0.05 a quarter, average V = 13.4×. There is no intercept. The band around a scenario is the model's typical one-quarter error, widened with the square root of the horizon.\n\n**How it was chosen, and how good it is.** About twenty candidate forms were compared on one-quarter-ahead forecasts, each fitted only on earlier quarters, across five different starting points for the test. The candidates varied how locked USD evolves (unchanged, momentum, momentum plus past returns) and what moves V (mean reversion, equities, the policy rate, combinations), and included ensembles. The findings:\n\n| | Typical error, given the equity path | vs no change | Typical error, equity path unknown | vs no change |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| No change | 27% | – | 27% | – |\n| Equity beta only | 26% | −5% | 27% | about 0% |\n| Driver model | 26% | −3% | 27% | about 0% |\n\n(16 forecasts, 2022 Q4 to 2026 Q3. Typical error is root-mean-square error in logs.)\n\n- **Without knowing where equities go, nothing beats \"no change\".** This held for every candidate.\n- Told the equity path, the equity-driven forms were 3% to 11% more accurate than \"no change\" in most test windows.\n- The ranking of candidates changed with the test window. Richer forms, with intercepts, the policy rate or momentum in locked USD, won in some windows and lost badly in others. That is what too little data looks like.\n- The form above was kept because it was the most stable, not because it was the best anywhere. Picking one form out of twenty by its test results flatters it.\n\nSo the driver model is a translator. It turns a view on the Fed and Wall Street into a crypto market cap. It does not supply the view, and a typical one-quarter error of about 26% says how loosely even a correct view maps to the outcome. The web app reruns this comparison on the current data and shows it under \"How good is the forecast?\".\n\n**Limits specific to these models.**\n\n- Stablecoin supply is the narrowest measure of locked USD. Direct fiat purchases and ETF inflows are missing, so V overstates the effect of a dollar of inflow.\n- The Treasury backing share is an assumption; no free series reports reserve composition.\n- V was far higher in 2019–2020, when the stablecoin base was tiny. That pulls its average up and makes κ small.\n- Turnover velocity, trading volume ÷ locked USD, is available live only. Its history has no free source.\n- The policy rate did not help forecasts reliably and is not in the V equation. The Fed enters through M2 only.\n\n## Current estimates\n\n| | Coefficient | t-stat |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intercept | −18.22 | −7.7 |\n| ln(M2) | 3.81 | 3.3 |\n| ln(US equity cap) | 1.80 | 4.2 |\n\nR² = 0.86, SE = 0.34 log points, n = 28, Durbin–Watson = 0.85, Engle–Granger τ = −2.45. Quarterly return beta of crypto to US equities = 2.2 (t = 3.3).\n\nThese are as of 2026 Q3. The live values are on `Params` and `Summary`.\n\n## Theory\n\n**Portfolio balance.** In Tobin's (1969) framework, money and other assets are imperfect substitutes: when the stock of money rises relative to what people want to hold for transactions, they rebalance into other assets and bid up their prices. Friedman (1988) applied the same idea to money and equity prices. This is the sound core of the original sheet's intuition. It implies a relation between asset *values* and the *stock* of money, which is why the model compares stocks with stocks and does not subtract flows from a stock.\n\n**Why the original \"excess liquidity\" was circular.** The quantity equation `M·V = P·Y` is an identity. If V is measured as GDP/M2, then GDP/V equals M2 by construction and `M2 − GDP/V` is zero. To get information out of it, velocity has to be anchored to something outside the current quarter. The P-star literature (Hallman, Porter and Small, 1991) does exactly this, using long-run velocity V* to define a \"money gap\". The overhang diagnostic follows that approach.\n\n**Crypto as a high-beta risk asset driven by US monetary conditions.** IMF research (Che, Copestake, Furceri and Terracciano, 2023) finds that one common factor explains about 80% of the variation in crypto prices, that its correlation with equities rose as institutions entered, and that Fed tightening lowers it much as it lowers the equity cycle. A follow-up (Copestake, Furceri and Terracciano, 2024) ties the equity co-movement to institutional participation. Iyer (2022) documents the post-2020 jump in crypto–equity correlation. This is why equity market cap is in the equation, and the 2.2 return beta in this dataset is consistent with it. Liu and Tsyvinski (2021) and Liu, Tsyvinski and Wu (2022) show crypto returns are driven by crypto-specific factors (market, size, momentum) more than by traditional macro factors, which is a reason to expect a wide error band around any macro fair value.\n\n**Why the model reports cointegration and out-of-sample tests.** Regressing one trending series on another produces high R² and large t-stats even when the series are unrelated (Granger and Newbold, 1974). A levels relationship is only meaningful as an \"equilibrium\" if the residual is stationary (Engle and Granger, 1987; critical values in MacKinnon, 2010). Here τ = −2.45 against a threshold of roughly −3.5 to −4.1, so cointegration is **not** established. Separately, Welch and Goyal (2008) showed that many predictors that fit in sample fail out of sample, which is why the signal is computed in real time and tested on the next quarter's return.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- **Small sample.** 28 quarters, 15 of them usable out of sample. Nothing here is statistically proven; the backtest can neither confirm nor rule out a modest effect.\n- **The M2 coefficient is not a pure liquidity effect.** M2 trends upward over the same period that crypto adoption grew, so `b1 = 3.8` absorbs adoption. An elasticity that large should not be extrapolated.\n- **No crypto-native variables.** Stablecoin supply, ETF flows, on-chain activity and leverage are absent. Adding them would likely help more than any further macro refinement.\n- **Possible structural break.** The 2026 gap may reflect a change in the relationship (the −43% reading is partly driven by record equity values), not mispricing.\n- **Real rate dropped from the equation.** It was insignificant with an unstable sign; it remains as a diagnostic column.\n- **Persistent residuals.** Durbin–Watson is well below 2, so the t-statistics on the coefficients are overstated and gaps last for several quarters.\n- **Data quality is uneven.** Full series could not be downloaded from FRED when the workbook was built. Values for 2025 Q1–2026 Q3 and the equity anchors were checked against the cited sources; most earlier values are rounded reference figures graded \"B\" (expected within about 1%), and some equity and gold-stock quarters are interpolated (graded \"E\", italic in the sheet). The equity \"corrections\" in `Audit_Log` are mostly a change of source and definition. Re-pull from the links in the [data dictionary](data-dictionary.md) before relying on any single cell.\n- **US real estate and \"private credit AUM\" were removed.** They could not be verified quarterly, and the private-credit figures were about 5–8× actual private-credit AUM (they match total private-markets AUM).\n- Not investment advice.\n\n## Possible extensions\n\nNone of these is implemented.\n\n- Add spot-ETF flows and exchange fiat inflows to locked USD, to widen it beyond stablecoins.\n- Replace the assumed Treasury backing share with figures from issuers' reserve reports.\n- Bring the out-of-sample comparison of forecasting methods into the workbook.\n- Replace the regression SE in the real-time Z-score with the forecast standard error.\n- Estimate an error-correction model if a longer sample ever supports cointegration.\n- Move to monthly data to raise the number of observations.\n\n## References\n\n- Che, N., Copestake, A., Furceri, D. and Terracciano, T. (2023). *The Crypto Cycle and US Monetary Policy.* IMF Working Paper 23/163. https://www.imf.org/en/publications/wp/issues/2023/08/04/the-crypto-cycle-and-us-monetary-policy-534834\n- Copestake, A., Furceri, D. and Terracciano, T. (2024). *The Crypto Cycle and Institutional Investors.* CEPR Discussion Paper 19810.\n- Engle, R. and Granger, C. (1987). Co-integration and error correction. *Econometrica* 55(2).\n- Friedman, M. (1988). Money and the stock market. *Journal of Political Economy* 96(2).\n- Granger, C. and Newbold, P. (1974). Spurious regressions in econometrics. *Journal of Econometrics* 2(2).\n- Hallman, J., Porter, R. and Small, D. (1991). Is the price level tied to the M2 monetary aggregate in the long run? *American Economic Review* 81(4).\n- Iyer, T. (2022). *Cryptic Connections: Spillovers between Crypto and Equity Markets.* IMF Global Financial Stability Note 2022/01.\n- Liu, Y. and Tsyvinski, A. (2021). Risks and returns of cryptocurrency. *Review of Financial Studies* 34(6).\n- Liu, Y., Tsyvinski, A. and Wu, X. (2022). Common risk factors in cryptocurrency. *Journal of Finance* 77(2).\n- MacKinnon, J. (2010). *Critical Values for Cointegration Tests.* Queen's Economics Department Working Paper 1227.\n- Tobin, J. (1969). A general equilibrium approach to monetary theory. *Journal of Money, Credit and Banking* 1(1).\n- Welch, I. and Goyal, A. (2008). A comprehensive look at the empirical performance of equity premium prediction. *Review of Financial Studies* 21(4).\n"}, {"slug": "workbook-guide", "title": "Workbook guide", "path": "docs/workbook-guide.md", "md": "# Workbook guide\n\nA reference for the layout of `crypto_model_v2.xlsx` (v2.2). For what the numbers mean see [methodology](methodology.md); for the quarterly routine see the [update guide](update-guide.md).\n\n## Colour conventions\n\n| Appearance | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Blue text | Input, typed in |\n| Italic blue text | Estimated input (interpolated or scaled; see the [data dictionary](data-dictionary.md)) |\n| Black text | Formula |\n| Green text | Formula that reads from another sheet |\n| Yellow fill | Cells meant to be edited: assumptions on `Params`, the scenario row on `Model` |\n| Green / red fill | Conditional formatting: Cheap or Easing or `OK` in green; Rich or Tightening or `CHECK` in red |\n\nUnits are USD trillions unless the column header says otherwise. Headers are bilingual (Chinese / English).\n\n## Sheets\n\n### `Guide`\n\nInstructions, how to read the output, limits, colour legend, version note.\n\n### `Summary`\n\nFour blocks, all formulas:\n\n| Block | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Latest reading | Actual, fair value, band, gap, both Z-scores, valuation signal, liquidity regime for the latest actual quarter |\n| Scenario row | Fair value and band under the scenario inputs; scenario gap if a crypto value is typed in |\n| How far to trust it | n, R², SE, Durbin–Watson, Engle–Granger τ, backtest correlation and hit rate, with plain yes/no readings |\n| Integrity checks | Nine checks and an overall status (see below) |\n| Liquidity chain | Flowing USD, free USD, locked USD, Treasury collateral, crypto V and market cap for the latest quarter |\n| Driver model, scenario row | Locked USD, crypto V and market cap one quarter ahead under the scenario inputs |\n\n### `Model`\n\nOne row per quarter from row 9. The row whose label contains `(scenario)` is the scenario row; keep that text out of every other cell in column B.\n\n| Columns | Group | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| B | | Quarter label |\n| C–L | Inputs | M2, ON RRP, GDP, CPI YoY, Fed funds target, public debt, US equity cap, crypto cap, gold price, gold stock |\n| M–T | Diagnostics | Gold market value, velocity, overhang ratio, excess money, real policy rate, Buffett ratio, crypto / M2, change in debt |\n| U–W | Regression variables | ln crypto, ln M2, ln equity |\n| X–AC | Fair value, full sample | Fair value, −1 SE, +1 SE, valuation gap, residual, Z-score |\n| AD–AJ | Real-time signal | Real-time fair value, real-time SE, real-time Z, valuation signal, 4-quarter rate change, liquidity regime, next-quarter crypto return |\n| AK–AO | Helpers | Residual change, crypto log return, equity log return, out-of-sample pair flag, directional hit |\n\n### `Flow`\n\nThe liquidity-flow chain and the driver model. Its rows match `Model` one for one: same row numbers, same scenario row. It reads `Model` by row number, so the two sheets must stay in step.\n\n| Columns | Group | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| B–F | Linked from Model | Quarter, M2, GDP, crypto market cap, US equity cap |\n| G | Input | Stablecoin supply (locked USD). In the scenario row it defaults to last quarter's lock-in share × the scenario's M2 |\n| H–K | The chain | Free USD, lock-in share, share of free USD, Treasury collateral |\n| L–P | Crypto V | V, ln V, its change, the change in ln equity cap, the pull toward V's running average |\n| Q–S | Driver model | V and market cap one quarter ahead given that quarter's equity move and locked USD, and the miss against the actual |\n\nColumns Q–S use coefficients fitted on the whole sample, so for past quarters they show fit, not forecasts. The out-of-sample test is in the web app.\n\n### `Params`\n\n| Rows | Content |\n|---|---|\n| 5–10 | Editable assumptions (yellow): V*, ounces per tonne, Z thresholds, regime threshold, minimum estimation window. Entries are validated |\n| 14–16 | Regression coefficients, standard errors, t-stats |\n| 18–24 | R², SE, observations, Durbin–Watson, Engle–Granger τ, return beta and its t-stat |\n| 27–31 | Range control: first data row, scenario row, last actual row, latest quarter, first real-time quarter. Formulas; do not edit |\n| 34–35 | Editable (yellow): share of stablecoin reserves held in Treasuries (an assumption), lookback for the usual range of V |\n| 36–42 | Driver model: β (equities), κ (pull to average), average V, median and quartiles of V over the lookback, observations |\n\n### `Backtest`\n\nCounts, average next-quarter return and share of gains by signal; correlation, t-stat and hit rate; a written verdict dated 16 November 2026. The verdict text is static and should be re-read against the numbers after each update.\n\n### `Charts`\n\n1. Actual crypto market cap vs fair value with the ±1 SE band\n2. Real-time Z-score\n3. Valuation gap, full sample\n4. Locked USD\n5. Crypto V\n\nChart ranges run through the scenario row, so a quarter inserted above it appears without editing the charts. The scenario row shows as the last point of the fair-value lines.\n\n### `Data_Sources`, `Audit_Log`, `Original_v1`\n\nSource and confidence grade per series, the stablecoin series included; the audit of the original sheet (14 logic problems, 87 data corrections); the original sheet itself. These are records and are not read by any formula.\n\n## Named ranges\n\nAll of them start at the first data row and end at `Params!C29`, the last actual row. They are what makes the workbook extend itself.\n\n| Name | Column on `Model` | Used for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Reg_Y` | U | Dependent variable, ln crypto |\n| `Reg_X` | V:W | Regressors, ln M2 and ln equity |\n| `Resid` | AB | Residuals |\n| `Resid_Lead`, `Resid_Lag` | AB | Residuals from the second row on, and up to the second-last row (Durbin–Watson, Engle–Granger) |\n| `dResid` | AK | Residual changes (Engle–Granger) |\n| `Ret_Crypto`, `Ret_Equity` | AL, AM | Quarterly log returns (return beta) |\n| `BT_Z`, `BT_Signal`, `BT_NextRet` | AF, AG, AJ | Backtest inputs |\n| `BT_OOS`, `BT_Hit` | AN, AO | Out-of-sample pair flag and directional hit |\n\nAnd on `Flow`:\n\n| Name | Column on `Flow` | Used for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Flow_Locked` | G | Stablecoin supply (the \"locked USD filled in\" check) |\n| `Flow_V`, `Flow_lnV` | L, M | Crypto V and its log |\n| `Flow_dV` | N | Change in ln V, from the third quarter on (dependent variable of the V equation) |\n| `Flow_X` | O:P | Its two regressors |\n\nThey are defined as, for example, `Model!$U$9:INDEX(Model!$U:$U,Params!$C$29)`. `INDEX` is used in preference to `OFFSET` because it is not volatile.\n\n## Formula conventions\n\n**Looking at another quarter.** A formula that needs the previous or next quarter finds it by position, not by a direct cell reference:\n\n```\nchange in debt      =H21-INDEX($H:$H,ROW()-1)\n4-quarter change    =G21-INDEX($G:$G,ROW()-4)\nnext-quarter return =INDEX($J:$J,ROW()+1)/J21-1\n```\n\nA direct reference such as `=H21-H20` breaks silently when a row is inserted between the two cells. The positional form does not.\n\n**Expanding window.** The real-time columns estimate on everything above the current row:\n\n```\n=IF(COUNT($U$9:INDEX($U:$U,ROW()-1))<Params!$C$10,\"\",\n    EXP(TREND($U$9:INDEX($U:$U,ROW()-1),$V$9:INDEX($W:$W,ROW()-1),V21:W21)))\n```\n\nThe window never includes the current row, which is what keeps the signal free of look-ahead.\n\n**Every row has the same formula.** Rows differ only in their own row number, so the row above can always be copied down. The first data row is the one exception: it has no previous quarter.\n\n**Blank, not zero.** Cells with nothing to report return `\"\"`. Totals and statistics are written to ignore them.\n\n## Integrity checks\n\nOn `Summary`. Each returns `OK` or `CHECK`.\n\n| Check | Fails when |\n|---|---|\n| Scenario row found | No label in `Model` column B contains `(scenario)` |\n| No blank inputs in actual quarters | An input cell in C–L is empty |\n| All inputs numeric | Text was typed into an input cell |\n| Level inputs positive | M2, GDP, equity cap or crypto cap is zero or negative |\n| Observations match rows | The regression is not using every actual quarter |\n| Residuals sum to zero | The fair-value columns and the regression are on different samples |\n| Minimum window is usable | The window is below 4 or leaves almost nothing to test on |\n| No formula errors on Model | Any calculated cell on `Model` shows an error |\n| Locked USD filled in | `Flow` lacks a stablecoin value for an actual quarter |\n\n## Compatibility\n\nThe workbook uses only functions available since Excel 2007 (`LINEST`, `TREND`, `INDEX`, `MATCH`, `SUMPRODUCT`, `COUNTIFS`, `AVERAGEIFS`). It has no macros, no external links and no volatile functions, and no formula depends on array entry. It is checked in both Excel and LibreOffice Calc.\n\nThe two do not always agree: up to v2.2 the scenario row was found with `SUMPRODUCT(MAX(...))`, which LibreOffice evaluates as an array and Excel does not, so Excel showed `#VALUE!` throughout. A new formula should be tried in Excel before it is relied on.\n"}, {"slug": "update-guide", "title": "Update guide", "path": "docs/update-guide.md", "md": "# Update guide\n\nHow to add a quarter, run a scenario and check the result, working in Excel. Layout details are in the [workbook guide](workbook-guide.md).\n\nThe [toolset](toolset.md) does the same from the command line and fetches most of the inputs for you. Whichever route you take, finish with `make update` so that the site follows the workbook.\n\n## Adding a quarter\n\n1. **Collect the eleven inputs** for the quarter that has ended: ten for `Model` and stablecoin supply for `Flow`. Definitions, sources and links are in the [data dictionary](data-dictionary.md). Use the same definitions as earlier rows (for example quarter-end month for M2, upper bound for the Fed funds target).\n2. **Insert a row** on `Model` directly above the yellow scenario row. Right-click the scenario row's number and choose Insert.\n3. **Copy the row above** into the new row (the whole row, columns B to AO).\n4. **Type the new values** over the copied ones: the quarter label in B and the inputs in C to L. Follow the colour convention: plain blue for sourced values, italic blue for estimates.\n5. **Repeat on `Flow`.** Insert a row above its scenario row, at the same row number as on `Model`, copy the row above into it, and type the stablecoin supply in column G. The two sheets must keep the same row numbers, because `Flow` reads `Model` by row.\n6. **Refresh the scenario row.** Change its label to the next quarter, keeping the text `(scenario)`, and set its yellow inputs. Clear its crypto cell unless you want a scenario gap.\n7. **Check `Summary`.** The latest quarter should be the one you added, and all nine integrity checks should read `OK`.\n8. **Update the records.** Add the quarter's sources and confidence grades to `Data_Sources`, and re-read the written verdict on `Backtest` against the new numbers.\n9. **Save, then check and republish:**\n\n```bash\nmake update\n```\n\nThis runs the independent check and rebuilds the site's data.\n\nNothing else needs editing. The regression, the driver model, diagnostics, backtest, `Summary` and charts all follow the last actual row.\n\nBecause two sheets must stay in step, `scripts/add_quarter.py` is the safer route: it does steps 2 to 6 in one go.\n\n## Running a scenario\n\nOverwrite the yellow cells in the scenario row on `Model`:\n\n- Leave **crypto blank** to see the fair value and band that the scenario's M2 and equity cap imply.\n- Type a **crypto value** to also see the scenario valuation gap.\n\nThe result is on the scenario row itself and in the scenario block on `Summary`. The scenario row never enters the regression or the backtest, whatever you type.\n\n## Changing assumptions\n\nEdit the yellow cells on `Params`.\n\n| Cell | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| V* anchor velocity | Changes the overhang diagnostics only |\n| Z thresholds | Reclassify quarters as Rich / Cheap / Neutral, and so change the backtest table |\n| Regime threshold | Reclassifies Tightening / Easing |\n| Minimum estimation window | Moves the first real-time quarter. A longer window gives more stable early estimates and fewer out-of-sample quarters |\n\nThe thresholds were set before looking at results. Tuning them to improve the backtest would make the backtest meaningless.\n\n## Revising history\n\nBlue cells can be overwritten at any time, for example when GDP is revised. Note that revising history changes the \"real-time\" columns too: they show what the model would have said given *today's* data for those quarters, not the data as first published.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Symptom | Likely cause |\n|---|---|\n| `Scenario row found` reads `CHECK` | The scenario label lost the text `(scenario)` |\n| `Locked USD filled in` reads `CHECK` | A row was added on `Model` but not on `Flow`, or its stablecoin cell is empty |\n| `Flow` shows the wrong quarter's figures | `Flow` and `Model` no longer have the same row numbers |\n| The latest quarter on `Summary` is not the one added | The new row was inserted below the scenario row, or a second label contains `(scenario)` |\n| `No blank inputs` or `All inputs numeric` reads `CHECK` | A missing value, or a number typed as text (often a trailing space or a `$` sign) |\n| `Observations match rows` reads `CHECK` | Crypto cap is blank in an actual quarter |\n| `#VALUE!` or `#NUM!` in the real-time columns | The minimum window is below 4, or an input is zero or negative |\n| The check script reports mismatches | The workbook was not recalculated and saved after the last edit, or a formula was overwritten with a value |\n| The check script finds no values at all | The file was last saved by a tool that does not calculate formulas. Open it in Excel and save |\n"}, {"slug": "toolset", "title": "Toolset", "path": "docs/toolset.md", "md": "# Toolset\n\nScripts in `scripts/` that keep the workbook, the data files and the site current. They need Python 3 and `openpyxl`; fetching also uses `curl`. A `Makefile` wraps the routine ones.\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `make check` | Recomputes the model independently and compares it with the workbook |\n| `make audit` | Compares the workbook's inputs with FRED, the Treasury and DefiLlama |\n| `make site` | Rebuilds the site's generated files |\n| `make update` | `check` and `site` in one go |\n| `make serve` | Serves the site at http://localhost:8765 |\n\n## Adding a quarter\n\n**1. Fetch what can be fetched.**\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/fetch_inputs.py \"2026 Q4\"\n```\n\nThis writes `data/quarters/2026Q4.json`. Seven of the eleven inputs come from public sources with no API key, one is estimated, and three are left empty:\n\n| Series | Source |\n|---|---|\n| `m2`, `gdp`, `cpi`, `rate`, `rrp` | FRED: `M2SL`, `GDP`, `CPIAUCNS`, `DFEDTARU`, `RRPONTSYD` |\n| `debt` | US Treasury, Debt to the Penny |\n| `stable` | DefiLlama, USD stablecoin supply (locked USD on the `Flow` sheet) |\n| `gold_t` | Estimated: previous quarter + 906 tonnes |\n| `equity` | **By hand**: Siblis Research |\n| `crypto` | **By hand**: CoinGecko quarterly report. The script prints today's live value as a guide |\n| `gold` | **By hand**: LBMA Gold Price PM, last fixing of the quarter |\n\nA series that is not published yet is left empty with the note \"not available yet\". GDP for a quarter first appears about a month after it ends.\n\n**2. Fill in the empty values** in the JSON file. List any series you estimated under `\"estimated\"`; the workbook shows those in italic blue.\n\n**3. Apply it.**\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/add_quarter.py --from-json data/quarters/2026Q4.json\n```\n\nOn both the `Model` and the `Flow` sheet, the script inserts a row above the scenario row, fills it and moves the scenario on by one quarter with inputs carried forward. It extends the conditional formatting, rebuilds the charts and then runs the check. A new quarter must be the one right after the latest and needs all eleven inputs. Use `--dry-run` to preview.\n\n**4. Publish.**\n\n```bash\nmake update\n```\n\n**5. By hand:** add the quarter's sources and grades to the `Data_Sources` sheet, and re-read the written verdict on the `Backtest` sheet against the new numbers.\n\nThe same steps can still be done in Excel, as the [update guide](update-guide.md) describes. Run `make update` afterwards either way.\n\n## Revising a value\n\nName an existing quarter and give only what changes:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/add_quarter.py --quarter \"2026 Q3\" --crypto 2.97\n```\n\n## The scripts\n\n### `check_model.py`\n\nReads the blue input cells, recomputes the regression, the real-time signal, the backtest and the driver-model equation in plain Python, and compares 29 results with the workbook's own. Exit code 0 means all match.\n\nA workbook saved by `add_quarter.py` holds formulas without calculated values, because `openpyxl` does not calculate. In that case the check lets LibreOffice calculate a temporary copy and checks that. Without LibreOffice, open the workbook in Excel, save it, and run the check again.\n\n### `fetch_inputs.py`\n\n`fetch_inputs.py \"2026 Q4\"` prepares a quarter, as above. `fetch_inputs.py --audit` compares every quarter in the workbook with the sources and lists values that differ by more than a tolerance: 1% for M2, GDP and debt, 2% for stablecoins, 5% for reverse repo, 0.15 points for CPI, exact for the policy rate. It changes nothing.\n\nOn 16 November 2026 the audit compared 196 values and found 3 differences, all in reverse repo, which is a memo series that no formula uses.\n\nThe script downloads with `curl` when it is installed. Python's own HTTP client was slow and unreliable against these hosts, and FRED drops connections from clients it does not recognise. A source that fails is reported and skipped, not retried.\n\n### `add_quarter.py`\n\nAdds or revises a quarter, from a JSON file or from flags such as `--m2 23.70`. It refuses a quarter that is neither existing nor next, a new quarter with missing inputs, and non-positive values where a logarithm or a division needs them.\n\n### `build_site.py`\n\nRegenerates `site/data/` and `site/downloads/` from the workbook and the markdown docs. It reads input cells only, so it does not need a calculated workbook. It warns if a quarter has no stablecoin value.\n\n### `wb.py`\n\nShared helpers: where things are in the workbook, reading inputs, shifting row references, building charts, calculating a copy with LibreOffice. Not a command.\n\n## Where the data lives\n\n| Data | Home | Maintained by |\n|---|---|---|\n| The ten macro and market inputs | The workbook, `Model` sheet, blue cells | `add_quarter.py`, or by hand in Excel |\n| Stablecoin supply | The workbook, `Flow` sheet, blue cells | `add_quarter.py`, or by hand in Excel |\n| Prepared quarters | `data/quarters/*.json` | `fetch_inputs.py`, then by hand |\n| Everything under `site/data/` and `site/downloads/` | Generated; do not edit | `build_site.py` |\n\nThe workbook is the single home for data. DefiLlama revises stablecoin history as it adds coins; `make audit` shows when a stored value has drifted from the source, and `add_quarter.py --quarter \"...\" --stable ...` applies a revision.\n\n## Known limits\n\n- Three inputs have no free source and are typed in by hand.\n- `add_quarter.py` was tested on a copy of the workbook, verified through LibreOffice. A workbook it has saved has not been opened in Excel as part of this work.\n- A workbook saved by `add_quarter.py` loses nothing that the workbook currently uses, but `openpyxl` does not preserve every Excel feature. If you add shapes, slicers or pivot tables in Excel, check that they survive.\n"}, {"slug": "web-app", "title": "Web app and portal", "path": "docs/web-app.md", "md": "# Web app and portal\n\nA static site in `site/`. It has no backend, no build step and no third-party libraries. Open `site/index.html` from disk, or serve the folder:\n\n```bash\nmake serve\n```\n\nthen visit http://localhost:8765. To publish it, upload the `site/` folder to any static host.\n\n## Pages\n\n| Page | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `index.html` | The portal: the chain today, the three macro lenses, the cross-check, downloads, links to everything else |\n| `forecast.html` | The forecast app |\n| `docs.html` | These documents, rendered from the markdown files |\n\n## The idea behind the forecast\n\nHow much USD can become crypto market cap? The app follows the dollars through a chain:\n\n| Step | Measure | 2026 Q3 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Flowing USD | M2 money stock | $23.4T |\n| 2. Free USD | `M2 − GDP ÷ V*`: money not needed to turn the real economy over at normal velocity | $0.64T |\n| 3. Locked USD | Supply of USD stablecoins: dollars that entered crypto through the primary market, by minting | $304B |\n| 3b. Treasuries behind it | Locked USD × the share of reserves held in Treasuries | $228B at 75% |\n| 4. Crypto V | Market cap ÷ locked USD: what secondary-market trading makes of each locked dollar | 9.7× |\n| Result | Crypto market cap = V × locked USD | $2.96T |\n\nThe equations and the evidence are in [methodology](methodology.md#liquidity-flow-model).\n\n## Using the forecast app\n\n**The chain today.** Six tiles, one per step. The slider underneath sets the Treasury share of stablecoin reserves, starting from the value on the workbook's `Params` sheet. That share is an assumption, because no free series reports reserve composition; it changes the Treasury figures and never the market cap.\n\n**Scenario.** Three ways to project, chosen with the buttons above the chart:\n\n| Mode | You set | The app works out |\n|---|---|---|\n| Driver model (default) | M2 growth (the Fed), US equity growth (Wall Street), any drift in the lock-in share | Locked USD and crypto V, from estimated equations |\n| Set lock-in and V yourself | M2 growth, the lock-in share and crypto V at the horizon | Nothing is estimated; it is the chain as pure arithmetic |\n| Cross-check: macro regression | M2 growth, equity growth, how fast today's valuation gap closes | The regression fair value from the workbook |\n\nEach mode has presets (recent trend, soft landing, risk-on, equity bear market, flat) and a horizon of 1 to 12 quarters. The results row, the two small charts (locked USD and crypto V) and the quarterly table follow the scenario.\n\n**The wider frame.** Three tables for the latest quarter:\n\n- **The Federal Reserve**: M2 and its growth, the policy rate and its change, the real policy rate, reverse repo, the money overhang, free USD.\n- **Wall Street**: US equity market cap, the Buffett ratio, crypto against equities and against gold, crypto's beta to equities.\n- **The Treasury market**: public debt, Treasuries held by stablecoin issuers, their share of the debt stock and of the year's new debt, and the interest issuers earn on them.\n\n**Bitcoin.** The model has no Bitcoin data. The app converts total crypto market cap into a Bitcoin market cap and price using two inputs: Bitcoin's share of the market and its supply. Both are placeholders until you press **Load live market data**.\n\n**Live data.** The button reads total market cap, Bitcoin price and dominance, and 24-hour volume from CoinGecko, and current stablecoin supply from DefiLlama. It then shows the chain live and reports turnover velocity (trading volume ÷ locked USD). M2 and GDP stay at the latest quarter, and scenarios still start from the latest quarter. Nothing else is sent anywhere, and the app works without it.\n\n**How good is the forecast?** A table comparing the driver model with \"no change\" and with a plain equity-beta rule, on one-quarter-ahead forecasts made out of sample. It is recomputed in the browser whenever the data changes. Read it before relying on any scenario.\n\n## How it is put together\n\n| File | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `site/data/model-data.js` | Inputs, stablecoin supply and parameters. Generated by `scripts/build_site.py` |\n| `site/data/docs.js` | The markdown docs. Generated |\n| `site/data/model-inputs.csv` | The inputs as a table, for download. Generated |\n| `site/downloads/` | A copy of the workbook. Generated |\n| `site/assets/model.js` | All the arithmetic: regression, real-time signal, backtest, liquidity flow, driver model, macro lenses |\n| `site/assets/charts.js` | Line and bar charts drawn as SVG |\n| `site/assets/site.js` | Header, footer, formatters, the chain tiles and lens tables shared by the pages |\n| `site/assets/style.css` | Styles, light and dark |\n\nThe same chain and driver model are in the workbook, on the `Flow` sheet, as live formulas. The out-of-sample comparison and the multi-quarter scenarios are in the app only.\n\nThe site holds data, not results. Every number on a page is computed in the browser from the same inputs as the workbook, so the site cannot drift from the model. For the latest quarter the browser's regression matches the workbook and `scripts/check_model.py` to at least nine digits.\n\nData is shipped as `.js` files, not fetched as JSON, so the pages also work when opened straight from disk.\n\n## After changing data or docs\n\n```bash\nmake update\n```\n\nThis checks the workbook and rebuilds the generated files. See the [toolset](toolset.md).\n\n## Limits\n\n- Stablecoin supply is the narrowest measure of locked USD. Dollars that bought coins directly or through ETFs are not in it, so crypto V overstates what one dollar of inflow does to market cap.\n- The Treasury backing share is an assumption.\n- Crypto V has ranged from 6× to 45× since 2019. Most of the uncertainty in any projection sits there.\n- The driver model's two coefficients come from 26 quarterly observations.\n- No scenario here is a prediction. Not investment advice.\n"}, {"slug": "data-dictionary", "title": "Data dictionary", "path": "docs/data-dictionary.md", "md": "# Data dictionary\n\nEvery input series: ten on the `Model` sheet and one on the `Flow` sheet. This mirrors the `Data_Sources` sheet as of 16 November 2026; if the two ever differ, the sheet is the record.\n\n## Confidence grades\n\n| Grade | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| A | Checked against the cited source on 16 November 2026 |\n| B | Reference value, rounded, expected within about 1% (10% for early crypto caps). Re-pull from the source before relying on it |\n| E | Estimated by the method described. Shown in italic blue in the sheet |\n\n## Series\n\n| Column | Series | Definition | Unit | Used in |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| C | M2 | Seasonally adjusted M2, last month of the quarter | $T | Regression, diagnostics |\n| D | ON RRP | Overnight reverse repo balance, last business day | $T | Memo only |\n| E | Nominal GDP | Current-dollar GDP, seasonally adjusted annual rate | $T | Diagnostics |\n| F | CPI YoY | CPI-U all items, 12-month % change, last month of quarter | % | Diagnostics |\n| G | Policy rate | Upper bound of the FOMC target range at quarter-end | % | Liquidity regime, diagnostics |\n| H | Total public debt | Total public debt outstanding, quarter-end | $T | Diagnostics |\n| I | US equity market cap | Market cap of all US-domiciled listed companies | $T | Regression, diagnostics |\n| J | Crypto market cap | Total crypto market cap, quarter-end | $T | Regression, backtest |\n| K | Gold price | LBMA Gold Price PM, last fixing of the quarter | $/oz | Diagnostics |\n| L | Gold stock | Above-ground stock | tonnes | Diagnostics |\n\nOnly M2, US equity market cap and crypto market cap drive the fair value. The policy rate drives the liquidity regime. Everything else is context.\n\n## Sources and confidence\n\n| Series | Source | Confidence by period | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M2 | Federal Reserve H.6 / FRED `M2SL` — https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/M2SL | A: 2020 Q3–Q4, 2025 Q3–2026 Q3. B: others | Sep 2026 = $23.41T (H.6 of 27 Oct 2026) |\n| ON RRP | NY Fed / FRED `RRPONTSYD` — https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/RRPONTSYD | B: all | Not in any model formula. Quarter-end days show window-dressing spikes |\n| Nominal GDP | BEA / FRED `GDP` — https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/GDP | A: 2025 Q2–2026 Q3. B: earlier | 2026 Q3 = Q2 level × 1.056^(1/4) from the BEA advance estimate (29 Oct 2026). 2024 Q2–2025 Q1 derived from published year-on-year rates. Earlier quarters are a pre-revision vintage |\n| CPI YoY | BLS — https://www.bls.gov/cpi/ | A: 2025 Q3, 2026 Q1–Q3. B: earlier | Mar 2026 3.3%, Jun 2026 3.5%, Sep 2026 3.7% |\n| Policy rate | Federal Reserve FOMC statements / FRED `DFEDTARU` | A: 2025 Q4–2026 Q3. B: earlier | Cut to 3.50–3.75% in Dec 2025; raised to 3.75–4.00% in Sep 2026; held 28 Oct 2026 |\n| Total public debt | US Treasury Debt to the Penny — https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/debt-to-the-penny/ | A: 2025 Q3, 2026 Q1, 2026 Q3. B: others | Includes about $7.8T of intragovernmental holdings, which do not absorb market liquidity |\n| US equity market cap | Siblis Research — https://siblisresearch.com/data/us-stock-market-value/ | A: year-ends 2019–2025, Jun 2025, Jun 2026, Sep 2026. E: other quarters | Estimated quarters scale the nearest published anchor by the S&P 500 price path, with the gap to the next anchor spread evenly. 2026 Q1 uses the S&P 500 change from Dec 2025 only |\n| Crypto market cap | CoinGecko quarterly industry reports — https://www.coingecko.com/research | A: 2025 Q1–2026 Q3. B: earlier | 2026 Q3 $2.96T; Q2 = Q3 less the reported $840.1B gain; Q1 = Q2 plus the reported $304.8B loss; 2025 Q4 from \"Q3 2026 is 4.0% below start of year\" |\n| Gold price | LBMA / World Gold Council — https://www.gold.org/goldhub/data/gold-prices | A: 2026 Q1 ($4,608), 2026 Q3 ($4,176). B: others | 2025 Q4 uses a reported year-end close of $4,323; 2026 Q2 is implied by a reported 3.7% Q3 gain to $4,157 |\n| Gold stock | World Gold Council — https://www.gold.org/goldhub/data/how-much-gold | B: year-ends 2019–2024. E: 2025 onward and all intra-year quarters | Intra-year values are linear interpolations; 2025–26 assume about 3,600 tonnes of net additions a year |\n\n## On the `Flow` sheet\n\n| Column | Series | Definition | Unit | Source | Used in |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| G | Stablecoin supply | Total circulating USD-pegged stablecoins, last observation of the quarter | $T | DefiLlama — https://stablecoins.llama.fi/stablecoincharts/all | Locked USD in the liquidity-flow and driver models |\n\nFetched on 16 November 2026 and matched against the source that day (grade A). It is a single-source series, and the source revises history as it adds coins; `make audit` shows any drift. It covers 2019 Q4 onward: $4B then, $304B in 2026 Q3. It has its own row on the `Data_Sources` sheet.\n\nThe share of stablecoin reserves held in US Treasuries is not a data series. It is an assumption on `Params` (75% by default).\n\n## Removed series\n\nUS real estate value and \"private credit AUM\" were in the original sheet and were dropped in v2. Neither fed the original result in a meaningful way, quarterly values could not be verified, and the \"private credit\" figures ($7.5T–$17T) match total private-markets AUM, not private credit (about $2–4T depending on definition). The original values remain on `Original_v1`.\n\n## Known weaknesses\n\n- The US equity series matters most, because it is a regressor, and 18 of its 28 quarters are estimates between published anchors.\n- Most values before 2025 are grade B. They are good enough for a regression on logs, where a 1% error in a level is small, but they should not be quoted individually.\n- GDP is a mix of vintages. It affects only the diagnostics.\n"}, {"slug": "changelog", "title": "Changelog", "path": "CHANGELOG.md", "md": "# Changelog\n\n## v2.2.1 — 16 November 2026\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **`#VALUE!` across the workbook in Excel.** `Params!C28`, which finds the scenario row, used `SUMPRODUCT(MAX(...))`. LibreOffice evaluates that as an array; Excel does not, and returned 0. Every named range ends at that row, so the regression, the diagnostics, the backtest and the driver model all failed. The cell is now `=MATCH(\"*(scenario)*\",Model!$B$1:$B$500,0)`. No value changes in LibreOffice.\n\n### Added\n\n- **`Data_Sources`**: a row for USD stablecoin supply (DefiLlama), with definition, link, grade and notes. Series labels now name their sheet, e.g. \"M2 (Model C)\".\n\n### Verification\n\n- Opened and fully recalculated in Excel for Mac: coefficients, diagnostics, backtest, driver model and scenario match the LibreOffice values, and all nine integrity checks read `OK`.\n- 29 of 29 checks pass.\n- Still not done: opening a workbook saved by `add_quarter.py` in Excel.\n\n## v2.2 — 16 November 2026\n\nThe liquidity-flow model is now in the workbook. The regression model and its numbers are unchanged: all 1,041 calculated values on `Model`, `Params` and `Backtest` match v2.1.\n\n### Added\n\n- **`Flow` sheet**: flowing USD, free USD, locked USD, lock-in share, Treasury collateral and crypto V for every quarter, and the driver model's one-quarter-ahead V and market cap, all as formulas. Its yellow scenario row works with the scenario row on `Model`.\n- **Stablecoin supply** as an eleventh input, on `Flow` column G, for all 28 quarters.\n- **`Params` rows 33–42**: the Treasury backing share and V lookback (editable), and the driver model's β, κ, average V, median and quartiles.\n- **`Summary`**: a liquidity-chain block, a driver-model scenario block, and a ninth integrity check (\"Locked USD filled in\").\n- **Two charts**: locked USD and crypto V.\n- Named ranges `Flow_Locked`, `Flow_V`, `Flow_lnV`, `Flow_dV`, `Flow_X`.\n\n### Changed\n\n- **The workbook is the single home for data.** `data/stablecoins.csv` and `scripts/fetch_stablecoins.py` are gone. `fetch_inputs.py` fetches stablecoin supply with the other inputs and includes it in the audit; `add_quarter.py` writes it and keeps `Model` and `Flow` in step.\n- `fetch_inputs.py` downloads with `curl` and no longer retries a failed source for every quarter. An audit takes about 15 seconds.\n- `check_model.py` also verifies crypto V, β, κ, average and median V, and the scenario's V: 29 checks.\n- The site takes the backing share and the V lookback from `Params`.\n- Adding a quarter by hand in Excel now means inserting a row on two sheets. `add_quarter.py` is the safer route.\n\n### Verification\n\n- Recalculated in LibreOffice with no formula errors; 29 of 29 checks pass.\n- β, κ, average V, the median and the quartiles in the workbook equal the web app's to at least nine digits.\n- `add_quarter.py` on a copy: a new quarter without `--stable` is refused; adding 2026 Q4 and revising a stablecoin value both pass 29 of 29.\n- `fetch_inputs.py --audit`: 196 values compared, 3 differ, all in reverse repo.\n- Not done: opening the workbook in Excel.\n\n## Site, liquidity-flow model and toolset — 16 November 2026\n\nThe workbook is unchanged at v2.1.\n\n### Added\n\n- **Web app and portal** in `site/`: static, no backend, no third-party libraries. A portal page, a forecast app and a docs viewer. All numbers are computed in the browser from the workbook's inputs.\n- **Liquidity-flow model**: flowing USD (M2) → free USD → locked USD (stablecoin supply) → crypto V → market cap, with the Treasury collateral behind locked USD carried alongside.\n- **Driver model**, the app's default forecast: locked USD follows M2, crypto V follows US equities and reverts toward its average. Selected by an out-of-sample comparison of about twenty forms; the comparison is rerun and shown in the app.\n- **Macro lenses** for the Federal Reserve, Wall Street and the Treasury market.\n- **Bitcoin** market cap and price implied by each scenario, from a dominance and supply assumption.\n- **Live data** in the app, on request, from CoinGecko and DefiLlama.\n- **Toolset**: `fetch_inputs.py` (FRED and Treasury, plus an audit mode), `add_quarter.py` (add or revise a quarter), `fetch_stablecoins.py`, `build_site.py`, and a `Makefile`.\n- `data/stablecoins.csv`: stablecoin supply for all 28 quarters.\n- Docs: `docs/web-app.md`, `docs/toolset.md`; new sections in `docs/methodology.md` and `docs/data-dictionary.md`.\n\n### Changed\n\n- `check_model.py` calculates a temporary copy with LibreOffice when the workbook holds no calculated values.\n- The README now describes the project as a whole.\n\n### Verification\n\n- The browser's regression, diagnostics and backtest match the workbook and `check_model.py`.\n- `add_quarter.py` was run on a copy: adding 2026 Q4 and revising 2026 Q3 both passed 23 of 23 checks. The real workbook was not modified.\n- `fetch_inputs.py --audit` compared 167 workbook values with FRED and the Treasury: 3 differ, all in reverse repo (memo only).\n- The site was exercised in a browser: three scenario modes, presets, live data, docs, phone width. No console errors.\n- Not done: opening a workbook saved by `add_quarter.py` in Excel.\n\n## v2.1 — 16 November 2026\n\nA restructuring of the workbook. The model, the input data and every reported number are unchanged from v2: all 994 calculated values on `Model`, `Params` and `Backtest` match v2.\n\n### Changed\n\n- **Estimation ranges follow the data.** The regression, diagnostics and backtest read from named ranges (`Reg_Y`, `Reg_X`, `Resid`, `BT_Z` and others) that end at the last actual row. In v2 these were fixed ranges such as `Model!$U$9:$U$36` that had to be extended by hand in 27 formulas each quarter.\n- **Cross-row formulas survive row insertion.** Change in debt, 4-quarter rate change, next-quarter return, residual change, log returns and the expanding estimation window now locate the neighbouring quarter with `INDEX(...,ROW()±k)`. In v2, inserting a row above the scenario row left the scenario row and the last quarter's next-quarter return pointing at the wrong cells.\n- **The minimum estimation window is live.** `Params!C10` now controls where the real-time columns start. In v2 the cell was documentation only.\n- **The next-quarter return ignores the scenario row.** In v2, typing a crypto value into the scenario row made the latest quarter show a \"realised\" next-quarter return. The guard keeps that out of the backtest now that its ranges are no longer fixed.\n- **Backtest statistics return `n/a`** when there are too few out-of-sample quarters, not an error.\n- **Update procedure.** One route only: insert a row above the scenario row and copy the row above into it.\n\n### Added\n\n- **`Summary` sheet**: latest reading, scenario, trust statistics and eight integrity checks.\n- **Range control block** on `Params` (rows 27–31).\n- **Helper columns** `AN` (out-of-sample pair) and `AO` (directional hit) on `Model`.\n- **Valuation-gap chart** on `Charts`.\n- **Conditional formatting** on the signal and regime columns and on the checks.\n- **Input validation** on the editable `Params` cells.\n- `scripts/check_model.py`, an independent recomputation of the model.\n- Project documentation: `docs/methodology.md`, `docs/workbook-guide.md`, `docs/update-guide.md`, `docs/data-dictionary.md`.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **Charts were shifted by one quarter.** In v2 each chart's value range started at row 8 (the units row) while its category range started at row 9, so every value was drawn against the following quarter's label. Both charts were rebuilt.\n\n### Moved\n\n- `docs/README.md` is now `README.md` at the repository root. Its theory, limitations and references sections moved to `docs/methodology.md`.\n\n### Verification\n\n- Recalculated in LibreOffice with no formula errors.\n- All v2 values reproduced.\n- `scripts/check_model.py` passes 23 of 23 checks on the shipped workbook, on a copy with a hypothetical extra quarter added, and on a copy with the minimum window set to 16.\n- Not opened in Excel as part of this work, and row insertion was tested by reproducing its effect in a script, not by inserting a row in Excel.\n\n## v2 — 16 November 2026\n\nRebuilt from `crypto.xlsx` (preserved as the `Original_v1` sheet).\n\n- Replaced the \"liquidity waterfall\" with a regression of crypto market cap on M2 and US equity market cap.\n- Added an error band, regression diagnostics, a real-time signal with no look-ahead, and an out-of-sample backtest.\n- Signals generated by formula; in the original they were typed by hand.\n- Corrected 87 input values and documented 14 logic and formula problems (`Audit_Log`).\n- Removed US real estate and \"private credit AUM\".\n\n## v1\n\nThe original `crypto.xlsx`.\n"}];
